      "/images/articles/article-executive-fitness-7smAuKbB8VnoomqQR4MdYo.webp",
    excerpt:
      "A practical, evidence aware fitness framework for busy executives over 40, covering strength, cardio, sleep, supplements, HRV and when to seek clinical help.",
    content:
      "After 40, the priority order is strength twice a week, easy cardio three times a week, and sleep protected above both. Supplements come last.\n\nStrength, aerobic fitness and recovery remain trainable after 40. Regular resistance training supports strength and function, while aerobic work supports cardiorespiratory fitness. Sleep, food and workload shape recovery.\n\nThis guide is a coaching framework, not a diagnosis or a promise of a particular result. A GP should review unexplained fatigue, chest pain, fainting, persistent pain, major sleep problems or symptoms that affect daily life. Get medical clearance before hard training if you have a relevant condition or take medicines that may affect exercise or supplements.\n\n## What changes with age?\n\nMuscle strength, power and bone density can change across adulthood, particularly when activity falls. The practical response is to train the capacities you want to keep: resistance exercise for major movement patterns, regular aerobic work and frequent walking. Build a routine that survives travel and busy periods.\n\n## What does a weekly training plan look like after 40?\n\nThe following is an **illustrative session template, not a real client result or a guaranteed prescription**. Adjust it to your training history, technique, schedule and health status.\n\n- **Day 1, full body strength:** squat or leg press, bench press or push up, row, hip hinge and a loaded carry or plank. Use controlled sets with a load that leaves a few good repetitions in reserve.\n- **Day 2, aerobic work:** 25 to 45 minutes at a conversational pace, such as brisk walking, cycling or rowing. Add short harder intervals only if you already tolerate them and have no relevant medical concerns.\n- **Day 3, full body strength:** split squat or step up, overhead press, pulldown or chin up progression, Romanian deadlift and an anti rotation core exercise.\n- **Other days:** easy walking, mobility work or complete rest. Keep at least one easier day between demanding strength sessions when possible.\n\nBegin with two strength sessions if three are unrealistic. Two consistent sessions are more useful than an ambitious plan that gets skipped. See our guide on [how to do a chin up](/insights/how-to-do-a-chin-up).\n\nChoose a weight you can lift with steady technique. A useful starting point is two to four sets of roughly 5 to 12 repetitions for most exercises. Power work needs extra care: use simple, low risk movements such as fast but controlled medicine ball throws or step ups, and stop before technique deteriorates. A coach can help select safer variations.\n\n## How do you progress without chasing numbers?\n\nTrack exercises, sets, repetitions, effort and symptoms. When repetitions feel controlled, add a small amount of weight, one repetition, or a set. Change one variable at a time. If sleep, work stress or joint symptoms worsen, reduce volume or intensity.\n\nProgressive overload means gradually asking the body to do more over time. It does not require maximal lifts. Good technique, repeatable sessions and appropriate recovery matter more than testing a one repetition maximum every week.\n\n## How much cardio do you need after 40?\n\nCardio trains the heart, lungs and working muscles. Choose activities you can repeat. A mix of easy aerobic work and occasional harder efforts can be useful, but the dose depends on fitness and medical context. VO2 max is informative, yet an association with health outcomes does not guarantee a particular lifespan or outcome.\n\nProtect a regular sleep opportunity. A common target is 7 to 9 hours, but sleep need varies. Keep wake time consistent, limit late caffeine and alcohol, and seek assessment for loud snoring, breathing pauses or persistent daytime sleepiness.\n\nSlow breathing may help you pause during a demanding day. In a randomised trial of five minute daily practice, mood measures improved, but HRV, resting heart rate and sleep did not change significantly. [Read the trial report](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9873947/).\n\nTreat wearable HRV as a personal trend. Sleep, alcohol, illness, training, stress and hydration can all affect it. Do not use one score to establish readiness or diagnose a problem. [Wearable HRV measurement has limits](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10742885/).\n\n## What should an executive over 40 eat, and is creatine worth it?\n\nEat enough food to support training. Include protein rich foods, vegetables, fruit, wholegrains and fluids. Protein targets should reflect body size, training, diet and medical needs. A dietitian can help with kidney disease, gastrointestinal illness or weight management concerns.\n\nCreatine is optional. The most studied form is **creatine monohydrate**. For a healthy adult who chooses to use it, a common maintenance approach is 3 to 5 g per day, with individual suitability considered. A loading phase is not mandatory, and there is no universal requirement to use 0.1 g/kg per day. The [International Society of Sports Nutrition position stand](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5469049/) and [NIH Office of Dietary Supplements guidance](https://ods.od.nih.gov/factsheets/ExerciseAndAthleticPerformance-HealthProfessional/) describe its strongest evidence in repeated, high intensity exercise and note that responses vary.\n\nChoose a product with transparent ingredients and independent, third party batch testing where available. Evidence for cognitive benefits remains uncertain. If you have kidney disease, abnormal kidney tests, are pregnant, or take medicines that may affect kidney function, discuss creatine with a clinician. Creatinine blood results may change with creatine use without proving kidney injury.\n\n## When should you see a doctor before training?\n\nA qualified strength coach can adapt exercise selection and progression. A GP, physiotherapist, sports physician, sleep clinician, psychologist or dietitian should be involved when symptoms, injury, medication, sleep breathing or medical conditions fall outside coaching scope. Seek urgent care for chest pressure, severe breathlessness, fainting or sudden neurological symptoms.\n\nFor a tailored starting point, use the [Performance Assessment](/assessment) or [apply](/apply) page. Any plan should begin with your history, current capacity, goals and constraints.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [International Society of Sports Nutrition position stand on creatine](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5469049/)\n- [NIH Office of Dietary Supplements: Dietary Supplements for Exercise and Athletic Performance](https://ods.od.nih.gov/factsheets/ExerciseAndAthleticPerformance-HealthProfessional/)\n- [Randomised trial of brief structured respiration practices](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9873947/)\n- [Review of wearable heart rate variability measurement](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10742885/)\n\n## Related resources\n\n- [Five movement training template](/resources/template)\n",
