    excerpt:
      "Periodisation gives busy adults a flexible way to plan training, manage fatigue and review progress without treating a template as a promise.",
    content:
      "Periodisation means planning training in phases of 3 to 6 weeks with a lighter week between them. It works for a 50 year old founder as well as it works for an Olympian.\n\n## What is periodisation?\n\nPeriodisation is the planned organisation of training over time. You vary the amount of work, effort, exercise selection and recovery emphasis so each block has a clear purpose. The plan can be simple. It may use a few weeks of skill and work capacity training, followed by a block that gives more attention to strength, then a lighter week.\n\nThis structure is useful when your schedule, sleep and stress change. It gives you a starting point for each session while leaving room to adjust the load or exercise. It is not a guarantee of results, and it is not the only sound way to train. A meta analysis found a moderate improvement in one repetition maximum strength with periodised programmes compared with non periodised programmes, but the studies mainly measured strength and included varied protocols. [Read the PubMed review](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/28497285/)\n\nRandom training is not automatically ineffective. Variety can be useful, and a short session is better than skipping movement. The problem is a pattern with no way to repeat key movements, add a suitable challenge or notice when fatigue is accumulating. A plan helps you make those decisions.\n\n## What are the four training phases?\n\nThese phases are a planning template, not a rule for every person:\n\n1. **Foundation:** Practise movement, build tolerance for regular training and establish manageable volume. Use loads that allow controlled technique.\n2. **Strength or intensification:** Keep the main movements familiar and gradually use more challenging loads or harder variations. Reduce work when needed so technique stays consistent.\n3. **Realisation or review:** Use a short block to express current strength or fitness. A rep quality set, submaximal estimate or timed test may be more suitable than a true one repetition maximum.\n4. **Transition:** Reduce training stress, retain easy movement and review what to change next. This can be a lighter week rather than complete rest.\n\nThe length of each phase depends on training age, goal, available time, injury history and recovery. A non athlete does not need an athlete’s peaking schedule. For many adults, repeating a small number of movements and adjusting effort from week to week is enough.\n\n## What does a 12 week periodised plan look like?\n\nThe following is **illustrative only. It is not a real client result or a prescription**. Change exercises, sets and effort to suit the person using it.\n\n| Weeks | Main aim | Example approach |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 1 to 4 | Foundation | Two full body strength sessions. Use goblet squats, push ups or presses, rows and a hinge. Add easy aerobic work if time allows. |\n| 5 to 8 | Strength emphasis | Keep the same movement patterns. Use fewer, higher quality repetitions and add load only when form and recovery support it. Include a third short session only if it fits the week. |\n| 9 to 10 | Review | Repeat selected movements or a simple submaximal test. Stop with good technique. Record effort, repetitions and any symptoms. |\n| 11 to 12 | Transition | Reduce sets or load. Keep walking, easy cycling, mobility and low effort skill practice. Plan the next block from the notes. |\n\nProgress does not require forcing heavier weights every session. You can add a repetition, improve range of motion, use a steadier tempo, reduce rest slightly or choose a harder variation. Keep one or two changes consistent long enough to judge them. If sleep is poor, work stress is high or pain changes your movement, reduce the session and reassess.\n\n## Which signs mean you need a lighter week?\n\nRecovery is part of the plan. Sleep, food, work demands and stress affect how much training you can tolerate. Wearable heart rate variability (HRV) is best treated as a personal trend measured under similar conditions. It is device specific and cannot diagnose illness or prove that you are ready, under recovered or safe to train. The [HRV guide](/insights/how-to-boost-your-hrv) should be used as a tracking aid, not a medical test.\n\nBreathing practice may help some people feel calmer. In a randomised study, daily structured breathing improved mood and reduced daily anxiety, and breathing rate fell more than in the comparison group. The study found no significant changes in HRV, resting heart rate or sleep measures. [Read the trial in PMC](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9873947/). Do not promise a fixed sleep, HRV or recovery improvement. Cold water exposure also has no guaranteed effect on readiness or performance.\n\nRegular activity supports physical and mental health, but associations are not personal guarantees. The [World Health Organization](https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/physical-activity) notes that any activity is better than none and that muscle strengthening benefits adults.\n\n## When should you see a professional?\n\nGet medical advice before starting or changing training if you have cardiovascular symptoms, a diagnosed condition, significant injury, pregnancy related concerns or medicines that affect exercise tolerance. Stop and seek urgent care for severe chest pain, fainting, severe breathlessness or sudden neurological symptoms. A qualified exercise professional can help with programming, but cannot diagnose or treat disease.\n\nFor a starting point, the [Performance Assessment](/assessment) can help organise training history, goals and constraints. If you want coaching support, you can [apply here](/apply). Any programme should be adjusted to your actual schedule, health context and response over time.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [Williams et al., “Comparison of Periodized and Non Periodized Resistance Training on Maximal Strength: A Meta Analysis,” PubMed](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/28497285/)\n- [Balban et al., “Brief structured respiration practices enhance mood and reduce physiological arousal,” PMC](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9873947/)\n- [World Health Organization, “Physical activity,”](https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/physical-activity)\n\n## Related resources\n\n- [Five movement training template](/resources/template)\n",
